    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d729">

                <pb facs="0884" n="796" sameAs="frus:frus1933v05/pg796"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">338.1121 Zrike Bros. and Johnson/79:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-11-02T11:00:00-05:00">November 2, 1933—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>116. Legation’s despatches number 172 and 174 of October 30.<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d729-f1"/> The Minister of Foreign Affairs has called at the
                    Legation in person and informed me that the Haitian Government desires to
                    withdraw its note concerning Johnson. I accordingly returned the note to him and
                    the incident may presumably be considered closed.</p>
                <p>The Minister of Foreign Affairs informed me that it is the present intention of
                    the Government to judge Johnson by default.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d729-f1" n="12" xml:id="d729-f1-content">
                <p>Despatch No. 174 not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
